00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.060
There we go.

00:01:04.060 --> 00:01:06.880
And live coding is always
much more exciting for you,

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:08.170
possibly, not for us.

00:01:08.170 --> 00:01:10.420
So we're going to get through
the slides very quickly.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:12.830
And then we're going to the
meat and the bones of the topic.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:13.330
OK.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.950
So for those of you
who may not know--

00:01:15.950 --> 00:01:18.450
there are very few of you in
the audience-- what is Flutter?

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:23.400
Well, Flutter lets
you build this.

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:26.880
This is an app, runs
in iOS and Android.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:29.633
And it's built from
a single codebase.

00:01:29.633 --> 00:01:31.050
But if you take a
look at the app,

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:35.640
it's not your standard out of
the box buttons and dropdowns

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:37.960
and app bars and
everything else.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:39.810
It has a pretty unique UI.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.840
And it's got some pretty
interesting animations.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:44.980
And really, that's
what Flutter is about.

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:48.013
It's not about building your
standard clone of an email app

00:01:48.013 --> 00:01:50.430
or building that chat app that
looks like every other chat

00:01:50.430 --> 00:01:51.060
app.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:54.960
It's about building apps that
could look like other apps

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:59.700
but give you the power to be
able to customize and to create

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.950
something that you and
your designers would want.

00:02:02.950 --> 00:02:05.970
So let's take a
quick look at why

00:02:05.970 --> 00:02:08.912
we think Flutter is good for
doing these types of things.

00:02:08.912 --> 00:02:11.370
Well, first off-- and you're
going to see the term "native"

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:11.880
here a lot.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:12.910
And it's very overloaded.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:14.035
So I'll try to be specific.

00:02:14.035 --> 00:02:15.870
And here, when I'm
talking about native UIs,

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:17.370
I'm talking about--
for those of you

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.670
with iPhones, when you're
scrolling something,

00:02:19.670 --> 00:02:22.170
you're scrolling behavior is
going to feel like your iPhone.

00:02:22.170 --> 00:02:23.130
It's going to drop down a bit.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.860
It's going to bounce
back up again.

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:26.442
And for those of
you on Android, it's

00:02:26.442 --> 00:02:28.150
going to have that
little inkwell effect,

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:30.240
that when you navigate
from screen to screen,

00:02:30.240 --> 00:02:33.020
the two platforms
behave differently.

00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:35.140
And so that's what we mean
by native UX, native UI

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:37.120
on each of those devices.

00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:38.870
And Flutter gives you
this out of the box.

00:02:38.870 --> 00:02:40.480
This is the same
app, same code base.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.100
But you see, they are
behaving in different ways.

00:02:43.100 --> 00:02:46.870
They're behaving appropriately
to the platform on which they

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:47.450
are running.

00:02:47.450 --> 00:02:48.783
And you get this out of the box.

00:02:48.783 --> 00:02:50.920
And Flutter takes
care of it for you.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.020
Quick, interesting
fact, everything you see

00:02:53.020 --> 00:02:55.060
here and in every
Flutter app, Flutter

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:57.610
is rendering 100% of
the pixels on screen,

00:02:57.610 --> 00:02:59.740
which means the buttons
and the app bars,

00:02:59.740 --> 00:03:02.293
they're not rendered by Android,
they're not rendered by iOS.

00:03:02.293 --> 00:03:03.460
They're rendered by Flutter.

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:06.280
And we have built a large
collection of widgets

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:07.540
to enable you to do this.

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:10.120
And I'll explain more why
we did that in a little bit.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:11.080
So we got native UIs.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.980
That's great.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:15.813
What we also have is we
have fluid and custom UIs.

00:03:15.813 --> 00:03:17.230
And this is one
of the reasons why

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:20.950
we control the rendering
because since we've built

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:23.090
our own approximations of--

00:03:23.090 --> 00:03:25.540
not approximations,
our own implementations

00:03:25.540 --> 00:03:30.550
of the Material design spec
and Apple's UI specification,

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:33.970
which we call Cupertino, we
are able to not only implement

00:03:33.970 --> 00:03:36.850
these but give you the ability
to heavily customize them

00:03:36.850 --> 00:03:39.270
if you so desire.

00:03:39.270 --> 00:03:41.720
And on top of that, because
we control the rendering,

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:44.780
we're able to optimize
these to be able to give you

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:47.480
the animations and the
60 frames per second

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:50.242
that you would expect.

00:03:50.242 --> 00:03:51.450
This is kind of a no-brainer.

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:53.075
If you've got one
code base, and you're

00:03:53.075 --> 00:03:55.920
building for two
platforms, you know,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.755
you're going to get half the
cost if the time is equivalent.

00:03:59.755 --> 00:04:01.350
And it's going to
cost you less money.

00:04:01.350 --> 00:04:02.610
But that's not
really the point here

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.530
because one of the
things that we have done

00:04:04.530 --> 00:04:07.440
and what we realize
is that giving you

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:10.440
an SDK out of the box is
only half the story because

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.040
for developers, what you need
is a really good set of tools

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:19.209
to be able to build apps
or software effectively.

00:04:19.209 --> 00:04:22.640
And so we have built
a suite of tools.

00:04:22.640 --> 00:04:24.480
And the one you see
up here is hot reload,

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.740
where when you edit a piece
of code and when you save it,

00:04:28.740 --> 00:04:31.470
it is deployed and rendered
to the device in which you're

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.420
testing, be it an emulator
or an actual device

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:37.080
plugged in, in near real-time,
a second, two seconds,

00:04:37.080 --> 00:04:38.520
maybe less than a second.

00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:42.137
And so your development cycle
is very, very, very iterative.

00:04:42.137 --> 00:04:44.220
And how does this translate
into what we in Google

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.360
have experienced?

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.790
Well, there is an app in Google
that we converted from iOS

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.290
and Android to Flutter.

00:04:49.290 --> 00:04:51.840
Took about three or four
weeks to get engineers up

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.370
to speed in the Flutter
framework to understand it.

00:04:54.370 --> 00:04:56.578
And once they did and started
to reimplement the app

00:04:56.578 --> 00:04:58.870
and then get to the point
where the app was implemented

00:04:58.870 --> 00:05:02.790
and add new features, it
was reported by metrics,

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:05.890
because we're Google, data, we
obviously gather a lot of it.

00:05:05.890 --> 00:05:08.310
And when we looked at it,
we saw that the developers

00:05:08.310 --> 00:05:11.370
were about two to three
times more productive

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.290
versus just one platform alone.

00:05:14.290 --> 00:05:18.260
And on top of that, they
also got the other platform

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.280
included in that.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:22.320
So we saw really significant
productivity gains,

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:23.242
which is why Flutter--

00:05:23.242 --> 00:05:24.950
or which is why Google
invests in Flutter

00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:28.225
because we get and see
real benefits from that.

00:05:28.225 --> 00:05:28.850
So there we go.

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:30.922
There's a little bit of
marketing spiel about why

00:05:30.922 --> 00:05:31.880
you should use Flutter.

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:32.630
That's great.

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:34.760
But let's look under
the hood a little bit

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.820
and see how some of these
things come to pass.

00:05:38.820 --> 00:05:41.520
Well, first of
all, Flutter's tech

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:46.180
stack, the entire UI stack is
built in the Dart language.

00:05:46.180 --> 00:05:48.060
There are many reasons
why we use Dart.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.660
And I'll cover a few
of them later on.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:51.990
But we have built the
entire UI stack in Dart.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:54.300
And because Flutter
is open-source,

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:56.520
all your developers
have full access

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.252
to the source code of that
UI, those UI elements, which

00:06:00.252 --> 00:06:01.710
means you can go
in and you can see

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.253
how we implemented a button.

00:06:03.253 --> 00:06:04.170
And you can take that.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:05.120
And you can change it.

00:06:05.120 --> 00:06:05.780
You can modify it.

00:06:05.780 --> 00:06:07.613
You can build your own
and be inspired by it

00:06:07.613 --> 00:06:08.740
or do something different.

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:10.573
But the entire source
code, it's like having

00:06:10.573 --> 00:06:13.020
the blueprint in front of
you to be able to implement

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.640
your own UI tools.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.005
As well as that, you
see we have layered it,

00:06:17.005 --> 00:06:18.630
which means if you
want Material or you

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:20.800
want Cupertino out
of the box, just use

00:06:20.800 --> 00:06:22.500
the widgets that we give you.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.000
But if you want to
go a little lower

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.320
and you want to change
the buttons to be

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.660
a little different or your
app bar behave differently,

00:06:27.660 --> 00:06:29.110
you can drop down a layer.

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:30.420
And you can customize there.

00:06:30.420 --> 00:06:32.160
Or you can drop
further, play around

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.250
with the different animations,
or drop even further and handle

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:37.230
all the painting and
rendering yourself,

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.680
which is why with Flutter, it
is very straightforward to be

00:06:40.680 --> 00:06:45.930
able to customize to a
very high degree your UI.

00:06:45.930 --> 00:06:47.640
So that's great.

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:51.210
Our engine is built in C++.

00:06:51.210 --> 00:06:54.870
And it runs on the Skia
rendering engine, which

00:06:54.870 --> 00:06:57.220
is an open-source project.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:59.280
And other projects, such
as Chromium, use it.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:00.130
And so we use this.

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:04.890
This is a highly-optimized
engine for drawing 2D graphics.

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:08.310
And it is available across
a large number of platforms.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:10.623
And the reason why we
use it is because there

00:07:10.623 --> 00:07:12.540
is a great implementation
of Skia for Android,

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.080
a great implementation of Skia
for iOS that we can compile to,

00:07:16.080 --> 00:07:17.220
we can compile to them.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:19.500
But because we have
an engine embedder,

00:07:19.500 --> 00:07:22.990
we can potentially deploy
to other platforms.

00:07:22.990 --> 00:07:25.980
So there is an experimental
engine embedder

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.855
for Flutter for Mac OS, which
you can go and get on our wiki,

00:07:29.855 --> 00:07:30.600
I think?

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:31.590
BRETT MORGAN: It's
on the GitHub, yep.

00:07:31.590 --> 00:07:33.132
MATTHEW SULLIVAN:
It's on the GitHub.

00:07:33.132 --> 00:07:35.700
There is a great
post we did in Medium

00:07:35.700 --> 00:07:40.310
which describes how to build
an engine embedder for Linux.

00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:43.620
So Flutter, at the moment,
we're focusing on mobile.

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:46.120
We're making sure the Android
and iOS experiences are great.

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:48.790
But there is the
potential to have Flutter

00:07:48.790 --> 00:07:51.040
run on other platforms as well.

00:07:53.253 --> 00:07:55.170
Another reason why we
use Dart, we didn't just

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.030
pick it out of a hat and go,
oh, there's the Dart language.

00:07:57.030 --> 00:07:57.913
We'll use that.

00:07:57.913 --> 00:07:59.580
One of the reasons
why we did it is Dart

00:07:59.580 --> 00:08:02.640
has a great compiler chain.

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:05.640
And so what that means is when I
showed you hot reload earlier--

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:08.550
and you're going to see
Brett use that heavily--

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:13.110
you saw code change and then
the actual implementation

00:08:13.110 --> 00:08:16.635
of the app change on the
device in near real-time.

00:08:16.635 --> 00:08:18.510
And the reason we can
do that is because Dart

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:19.770
has a virtual machine.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:22.307
And when you're in debug
mode, we can take your app,

00:08:22.307 --> 00:08:23.140
put it on the phone.

00:08:23.140 --> 00:08:25.540
We can take your virtual
machine, put it on the phone.

00:08:25.540 --> 00:08:26.700
And every time you
change your code,

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.117
we can inject that code straight
into the virtual machine.

00:08:29.117 --> 00:08:31.110
And boom, it's
running on the device.

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:33.299
That sounds wonderful,
but virtual machines

00:08:33.299 --> 00:08:35.340
come at a cost in
terms of performance

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:38.010
and come at a cost in terms
of the size of your app, which

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.830
isn't ideal when you're going
to be deploying to production.

00:08:40.830 --> 00:08:44.039
But Dart gives this other means
of being able to compile it.

00:08:44.039 --> 00:08:45.870
So that was our
[INAUDIBLE] implementation.

00:08:45.870 --> 00:08:48.450
We have ahead-of-time compiler,
which will take your Flutter

00:08:48.450 --> 00:08:50.550
and Dart code and
will compile it down

00:08:50.550 --> 00:08:53.200
to native machine
code on the device.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:55.890
We have our machine
code on iOS and Android.

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:57.630
And it is running
on the bare metal.

00:08:57.630 --> 00:08:59.400
We don't need a
VM at that point.

00:08:59.400 --> 00:09:00.960
We don't need a
lot of the tooling

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:03.090
we've built for debugging
and profiling your app.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:04.500
We strip it all out.

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.340
And it becomes
much, much smaller.

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:07.920
So you get the best
of both worlds.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:10.510
Dart gives us the
ability to go, hey,

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.240
give us an amazing
developer experience

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.460
and, hey, streamline
us to the n-th degree

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:22.170
so we can run as fast as we
can for users in production.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:24.840
And developer tools, if
you use Android Studio

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:26.610
IntelliJ-- fantastic plugin.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:27.900
We maintain it ourselves.

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:31.020
If you use, like myself,
Visual Studio Code, fantastic

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:32.700
plugin which we
maintain ourselves.

00:09:32.700 --> 00:09:35.400
But the infrastructure we've
built for these platforms

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.210
is available for the developers.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.280
So if you want to go
out there and build

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:43.788
an integration
for vim, which has

00:09:43.788 --> 00:09:46.080
been done by some of the
engineers on the Flutter team,

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:47.455
or you want to
build it for Emacs

00:09:47.455 --> 00:09:51.485
or any of the other IDEs,
that's entirely possible.

00:09:51.485 --> 00:09:53.110
We have full terminal
support for those

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:54.652
who like to live in
the command line,

00:09:54.652 --> 00:09:57.750
for those who like to have
continuous integration servers.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:09:59.670
I've mentioned hot reload.

00:09:59.670 --> 00:10:01.170
And we have a great
package manager.

00:10:01.170 --> 00:10:03.587
So when you're pulling in your
first-party and third-party

00:10:03.587 --> 00:10:05.078
packages and
libraries and plugins,

00:10:05.078 --> 00:10:06.870
we are able to take
care of the versioning,

00:10:06.870 --> 00:10:10.795
pull in the right pieces
for you to make that happen.

00:10:10.795 --> 00:10:12.420
We have platform
channels, which allows

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:16.350
you to integrate with
existing Android and iOS code,

00:10:16.350 --> 00:10:19.595
be it Java, Kotlin,
Swift, and Objective--

00:10:19.595 --> 00:10:20.220
I got them all.

00:10:20.220 --> 00:10:22.023
BRETT MORGAN: Objective C, yep.

00:10:22.023 --> 00:10:23.940
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: And
we can go the other way.

00:10:23.940 --> 00:10:26.480
And you can add Flutter
to existing apps.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:28.980
So for those of you out there
who go, this sounds fantastic.

00:10:28.980 --> 00:10:29.490
Flutter is great.

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:31.157
But we've already
invested years of time

00:10:31.157 --> 00:10:34.540
building our apps
on both platforms.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.090
You are able to integrate that.

00:10:36.090 --> 00:10:37.710
And we've seen a
lot of people take

00:10:37.710 --> 00:10:39.600
that route, where they
have a small portion

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.638
of their app in Flutter.

00:10:40.638 --> 00:10:42.180
They tie it into
their existing apps.

00:10:42.180 --> 00:10:45.000
Then over time, they can
transfer more or less of it

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:47.340
into Flutter and still get,
for at least that portion,

00:10:47.340 --> 00:10:51.220
a single code base
on which to run it.

00:10:51.220 --> 00:10:54.642
I'm going to very quickly talk
about how UIs are composed

00:10:54.642 --> 00:10:57.100
because Brett's going to be
diving through a bunch of code.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.170
And I just want to give
you a flavor now so you

00:10:59.170 --> 00:11:01.330
don't get lost in the rush.

00:11:01.330 --> 00:11:04.280
Here is an app.

00:11:04.280 --> 00:11:06.280
It's got some images and
some icons and whatnot.

00:11:06.280 --> 00:11:09.030
But how is Flutter actually--
how do you as a developer

00:11:09.030 --> 00:11:12.260
or how are your developers
going to build for this?

00:11:12.260 --> 00:11:14.560
Well, let's take a look.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:15.060
OK.

00:11:15.060 --> 00:11:19.200
So these things seem to be laid
out vertically to begin with.

00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:22.120
So Flutter has the
concept of a column.

00:11:22.120 --> 00:11:25.227
And we can wrap our UI
widgets in a column.

00:11:25.227 --> 00:11:26.310
And then here's the thing.

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:30.090
Pretty much everything in
Flutter, bar a few exceptions,

00:11:30.090 --> 00:11:30.722
are widgets.

00:11:30.722 --> 00:11:32.430
And typically, when
you think of widgets,

00:11:32.430 --> 00:11:37.740
you go UI components,
button, app bar, icon, text.

00:11:37.740 --> 00:11:40.810
But in Flutter, layout
is also a widget.

00:11:40.810 --> 00:11:43.260
So in your code, you're
actually defining

00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:45.510
the layout, which can
be jarring for people

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:46.800
who are coming from--

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:50.100
excuse me, coming from
things like Xamlon Windows

00:11:50.100 --> 00:11:52.380
or XML layouts in
Android because you're

00:11:52.380 --> 00:11:54.150
used to that separation.

00:11:54.150 --> 00:11:56.670
But what we've found
is because of the way

00:11:56.670 --> 00:11:59.130
Dart structures its code
and because of the way

00:11:59.130 --> 00:12:02.400
the Flutter SDK is
structured, it is actually

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:05.130
very readable and
very maintainable

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:08.190
and gives you things
like hot reload

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.010
and very maintainable
for the code given.

00:12:11.010 --> 00:12:13.620
So there we go, vertical,
wrap it in a column.

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:14.340
That's great.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.080
Well, the thing at
the top of the column

00:12:16.080 --> 00:12:17.190
is probably an image.

00:12:17.190 --> 00:12:18.670
That's not too interesting.

00:12:18.670 --> 00:12:20.007
But then we take a look at this.

00:12:20.007 --> 00:12:21.340
And this is kind of interesting.

00:12:21.340 --> 00:12:22.798
There's a few things
going in here.

00:12:22.798 --> 00:12:24.370
So let's take a closer look.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:26.940
So it looks like we have
our text on one side.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:30.570
And we have an icon and a
number on the other side.

00:12:30.570 --> 00:12:32.070
So that's a horizontal layout.

00:12:32.070 --> 00:12:33.690
We can wrap that in a row.

00:12:33.690 --> 00:12:36.430
It looks like our text
expands to fill the space.

00:12:36.430 --> 00:12:38.940
So we can wrap that
in an expanded.

00:12:38.940 --> 00:12:40.493
And then if you
think about the text,

00:12:40.493 --> 00:12:42.660
there's one on top of the
other, internally in that,

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.410
we can then wrap it in a column.

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:46.710
So you've got columns
and rows and columns.

00:12:46.710 --> 00:12:48.300
And we have things like stacks.

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:51.060
And these may seem
very primitive

00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.700
compared to potentially some
of the other types of layouts

00:12:53.700 --> 00:12:54.450
you get elsewhere.

00:12:54.450 --> 00:12:56.520
But we've found that just
using these primitives

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:59.970
and using them cleverly, we
are able to construct very

00:12:59.970 --> 00:13:02.100
sophisticated UIs from these.

00:13:02.100 --> 00:13:04.980
And it's very
straightforward to do that.

00:13:04.980 --> 00:13:06.680
So when you look at
it, all you really

00:13:06.680 --> 00:13:08.660
have here is a column of things.

00:13:08.660 --> 00:13:11.660
And then when you come down to
your third row, for example,

00:13:11.660 --> 00:13:13.670
it looks like you
have a row of icons.

00:13:13.670 --> 00:13:15.853
And each of those is a
column with one or the other.

00:13:15.853 --> 00:13:17.270
And that's how we
build things up.

00:13:17.270 --> 00:13:20.600
We compose our UI
elements together.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:25.690
And we lay them out
with the same code.

00:13:25.690 --> 00:13:28.180
We have a full
set implementation

00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:32.230
of Material design for
Flutter, unsurprisingly.

00:13:32.230 --> 00:13:35.650
In fact our Material design
spec is highly inclusive.

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:38.692
We pretty much cover everything
that the spec defines.

00:13:38.692 --> 00:13:39.400
So you can go in.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:43.160
And you can build Material
design style apps very,

00:13:43.160 --> 00:13:43.942
very easily.

00:13:43.942 --> 00:13:45.400
And because the
new Material design

00:13:45.400 --> 00:13:47.350
is designed to be
fairly flexible,

00:13:47.350 --> 00:13:49.750
Flutter is ideally placed
to give you that flexibility

00:13:49.750 --> 00:13:52.060
to be able to customize
and tweak these.

00:13:52.060 --> 00:13:55.510
But sometimes you just want to
build an app that looks more

00:13:55.510 --> 00:14:00.140
like a standard iOS app,
which is why out of the box

00:14:00.140 --> 00:14:02.830
we also offer what we call
Cupertino widgets, which will

00:14:02.830 --> 00:14:05.530
let you compose things
and put things together

00:14:05.530 --> 00:14:09.237
in terms of more of
Apple's style of widgets.

00:14:09.237 --> 00:14:10.570
And you can mix and match these.

00:14:10.570 --> 00:14:12.020
And you can use
one or the other.

00:14:12.020 --> 00:14:15.400
We're don't specify
it's all or nothing.

00:14:15.400 --> 00:14:17.330
But we give you
these out of the box

00:14:17.330 --> 00:14:19.600
so you have complete
choice as to how

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:23.290
you want to build your apps.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:27.930
Two or three more slides, and
then Brett's on the spotlight.

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.520
But I want to talk very
briefly about cross platform

00:14:30.520 --> 00:14:32.420
and our ecosystem.

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:36.162
So as I mentioned, if you have
a clump of Kotlin code or Java

00:14:36.162 --> 00:14:37.870
code, and you have a
bunch of Swift code,

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:40.330
and you're like I've spent
ages building this code.

00:14:40.330 --> 00:14:41.830
It does a lot of
the business logic.

00:14:41.830 --> 00:14:43.000
It's fundamental to my business.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:44.350
I don't want to rewrite this.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:47.110
But I would really love to
use some of Flutter's UI

00:14:47.110 --> 00:14:49.930
capabilities or extend
it with Flutter and Dart,

00:14:49.930 --> 00:14:51.250
you can do that.

00:14:51.250 --> 00:14:54.040
So we have the ability to
be able to do cross platform

00:14:54.040 --> 00:14:57.710
RPC in both directions and
even have event channels

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:00.020
so you can stream events in
one direction or the other.

00:15:00.020 --> 00:15:05.860
So you can have Android and
iOS code talk to Flutter code.

00:15:05.860 --> 00:15:09.200
And as I said, you can do it
in the other direction as well.

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:12.110
We also have a great
ecosystem of packages.

00:15:12.110 --> 00:15:13.720
Some of which we
maintain ourselves,

00:15:13.720 --> 00:15:16.760
like Firebase plugins are us,
the WebView and Maps plugins

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:17.260
are us.

00:15:17.260 --> 00:15:18.550
We do Camera.

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:20.050
We do a bunch of
things that we feel

00:15:20.050 --> 00:15:21.640
are critical to the platform.

00:15:21.640 --> 00:15:24.040
But the community has
stepped up and added

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:27.460
tons of other packages
and plugins out there.

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:28.890
And you can go to that website.

00:15:28.890 --> 00:15:31.610
In fact, you can just do
Flutter Packages, Google that.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:32.860
And you will get to this site.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:35.800
And you can just see
the range of packages

00:15:35.800 --> 00:15:37.730
that are available to
you out of the box,

00:15:37.730 --> 00:15:40.810
which will save
you time building.

00:15:40.810 --> 00:15:44.700
But the idea here is that
Dart's been around for a while.

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:46.180
Flutter's fairly new.

00:15:46.180 --> 00:15:49.650
But we already have a rich
ecosystem of Dart code.

00:15:49.650 --> 00:15:52.350
And our Flutter ecosystem
is growing steadily

00:15:52.350 --> 00:15:56.040
to the point where we have
over 1,400 Flutter packages

00:15:56.040 --> 00:16:00.060
in our pub system today.

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:02.370
Flutter is completely
open-source.

00:16:02.370 --> 00:16:05.880
Every line of code is in
github.com/flutter/flutter

00:16:05.880 --> 00:16:08.062
or github.com/flutter/engine.

00:16:08.062 --> 00:16:08.770
You can go there.

00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:10.410
You can see how
everything is built.

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.560
And we take contributions
externally all the time.

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:17.910
There's over 300 contributors to
the Flutter repository and code

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:20.080
base itself.

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:23.160
In fact, we've gotten so popular
that we're ranked pretty highly

00:16:23.160 --> 00:16:27.260
now in terms of popularity
for GitHub repos.

00:16:27.260 --> 00:16:29.622
There are thousands
of apps written

00:16:29.622 --> 00:16:31.580
in Flutter that have been
published to the Play

00:16:31.580 --> 00:16:33.575
Store and the App Store.

00:16:33.575 --> 00:16:34.700
Google has published a few.

00:16:34.700 --> 00:16:36.750
The community has
published thousands more.

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:38.750
We'll show you-- we'll
give you a taste of who's

00:16:38.750 --> 00:16:40.610
doing what towards the end.

00:16:40.610 --> 00:16:43.590
But Flutter is ready for
use in production today.

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:46.238
It has been for a long time.

00:16:46.238 --> 00:16:47.780
The app we always
used to talk about,

00:16:47.780 --> 00:16:50.410
Hamilton, is like
a year and a half

00:16:50.410 --> 00:16:53.102
more since it was
first put out there.

00:16:53.102 --> 00:16:54.560
And since then,
we've gone to beta.

00:16:54.560 --> 00:16:59.060
We're at 1.0, we're at 1.2
at the moment, I believe.

00:16:59.060 --> 00:17:03.320
And so we have a solid, stable
platform that we in Google

00:17:03.320 --> 00:17:07.310
rely on and that we feel that
you can rely on to provide

00:17:07.310 --> 00:17:10.750
services to your users.

00:17:10.750 --> 00:17:11.932
Our community is awesome.

00:17:11.932 --> 00:17:12.849
We love our community.

00:17:12.849 --> 00:17:13.807
They're very inclusive.

00:17:13.807 --> 00:17:16.300
And they have a ton
of areas in which you

00:17:16.300 --> 00:17:17.500
can start with Flutter.

00:17:17.500 --> 00:17:19.752
If you go to
flutter.dev/community,

00:17:19.752 --> 00:17:21.460
we have a bunch of
community links there.

00:17:21.460 --> 00:17:23.530
We do things like
startflutter.com,

00:17:23.530 --> 00:17:24.490
flutter.institute.

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:27.280
We have fantastic documentation,
I feel, on flutter.dev.

00:17:27.280 --> 00:17:30.640
The community has
produced so much more.

00:17:30.640 --> 00:17:32.260
So if you want to
learn about Flutter,

00:17:32.260 --> 00:17:34.010
there is some starting points.

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:36.820
But I think I have
waffled on enough.

00:17:36.820 --> 00:17:40.360
So without further ado,
what could possibly go wrong

00:17:40.360 --> 00:17:41.590
with your live coding demo?

00:17:41.590 --> 00:17:42.387
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:42.387 --> 00:17:44.845
BRETT MORGAN: So if we could
transfer to the demo computer,

00:17:44.845 --> 00:17:48.730
and let's see how much of a fool
I can make of myself on stage.

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:49.970
Here we are.

00:17:49.970 --> 00:17:50.840
So here we are.

00:17:50.840 --> 00:17:54.670
We're starting with your
traditional hello world app.

00:17:54.670 --> 00:17:58.090
What we're going to build
today is an application

00:17:58.090 --> 00:18:02.180
to let us roam around the ice
cream stores of San Francisco.

00:18:02.180 --> 00:18:04.900
It seemed like a great idea
while I was sweltering away

00:18:04.900 --> 00:18:06.050
in the Australian heat.

00:18:06.050 --> 00:18:07.675
But now that I've
come to San Francisco

00:18:07.675 --> 00:18:09.717
and it's all kinds of
cold, and I'm sort of like,

00:18:09.717 --> 00:18:12.072
I should have done
coffee instead.

00:18:12.072 --> 00:18:13.030
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: Yeah.

00:18:13.030 --> 00:18:15.155
There's a reason why I have
my woolly hat on today.

00:18:15.155 --> 00:18:16.900
BRETT MORGAN: Yeah, totally.

00:18:16.900 --> 00:18:20.080
So, growing up in
Australia, ice cream stores

00:18:20.080 --> 00:18:22.240
use this palette
of pastel colors.

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:25.162
And so blue and white, it's
not really doing it for me.

00:18:25.162 --> 00:18:26.870
So the first thing
we're going to change,

00:18:26.870 --> 00:18:28.890
so we're going to
change the color.

00:18:28.890 --> 00:18:31.740
And what you've seen there is
I've made a change to the code,

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:33.310
saved it to disk.

00:18:33.310 --> 00:18:34.490
Flutter has noticed.

00:18:34.490 --> 00:18:36.093
And it's hot reloaded the app.

00:18:36.093 --> 00:18:37.510
And as you saw,
it's pretty quick.

00:18:40.280 --> 00:18:44.100
So the obvious
question is, if I've

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:47.440
got a list of ice cream stores,
where are we going to store it?

00:18:47.440 --> 00:18:50.360
Well, being a cloud
conference, of course,

00:18:50.360 --> 00:18:53.330
I'm going to go with
Google Cloud Firestore.

00:18:53.330 --> 00:18:55.820
And the first step in
including functionality

00:18:55.820 --> 00:18:59.880
is we import it at
the top of the file.

00:18:59.880 --> 00:19:02.870
Now, the next step is I'm
going to turn around and make

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:04.820
a quick change to
the application.

00:19:04.820 --> 00:19:08.060
And something really
amazing is going to happen.

00:19:08.060 --> 00:19:11.160
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: You-- in two
minutes, you broke the app.

00:19:11.160 --> 00:19:11.660
Well done.

00:19:11.660 --> 00:19:12.220
Congratulations.

00:19:12.220 --> 00:19:13.130
Round of applause for my--

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:13.520
BRETT MORGAN: OK, I'm done.

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:14.000
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: --the app.

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:14.360
Yay!

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:14.890
There we go.

00:19:14.890 --> 00:19:16.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:19:16.820 --> 00:19:19.480
BRETT MORGAN: So before
we run away in horror,

00:19:19.480 --> 00:19:22.000
it's actually trying to tell
us something interesting.

00:19:22.000 --> 00:19:25.580
Type HomePage is not a
subtype of StatelessWidget.

00:19:25.580 --> 00:19:29.880
So what's happened
here is initially,

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.720
this was a StatelessWidget.

00:19:32.720 --> 00:19:34.640
I'm hoping that wasn't
my phone beeping.

00:19:34.640 --> 00:19:37.220
Anyway, it was a
StatelessWidget.

00:19:37.220 --> 00:19:39.110
And we've turned it
into a StatefulWidget

00:19:39.110 --> 00:19:42.140
because I need it to
actually have some state.

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:44.390
And Flutter has
gone, I can't quite

00:19:44.390 --> 00:19:46.860
figure out what you're doing
here, help me out here.

00:19:46.860 --> 00:19:49.150
And the fairly easy
way of helping it out

00:19:49.150 --> 00:19:51.020
is turning around
and reloading it.

00:19:51.020 --> 00:19:52.520
And we're up and running again.

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:56.330
Again, reloading
takes longer than--

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:59.080
sorry-- am I using my
phrase incorrectly?

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:00.330
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: [INAUDIBLE].

00:20:00.330 --> 00:20:02.872
BRETT MORGAN: Reloading is a
bit slower, but not that slower.

00:20:02.872 --> 00:20:04.955
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: So one
of the things to see here

00:20:04.955 --> 00:20:06.315
is that, like, the app broke.

00:20:06.315 --> 00:20:09.180
And it's not
immediately obvious why.

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:11.670
And that's because one of
the benefits of hot reload

00:20:11.670 --> 00:20:13.450
is that it maintains state.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:15.600
So if you're five
screens down in your app

00:20:15.600 --> 00:20:17.790
and you're tweaking
your UI and whatnot,

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:19.560
you don't have to
renavigate back there

00:20:19.560 --> 00:20:21.137
every time you save your code.

00:20:21.137 --> 00:20:23.220
What happened here, though,
is that Brett actually

00:20:23.220 --> 00:20:25.503
changed the code which
maintains the state.

00:20:25.503 --> 00:20:28.170
And once you do that, Flutter is
not smart enough to kind of go,

00:20:28.170 --> 00:20:30.810
I'm good, and that automagically
make your state recover.

00:20:30.810 --> 00:20:33.620
And so we have to do
a hot restart, which--

00:20:33.620 --> 00:20:34.620
BRETT MORGAN: Thank you.

00:20:34.620 --> 00:20:38.040
MATTHEW SULLIVAN: --is
a second and a half

00:20:38.040 --> 00:20:39.840
versus 700 milliseconds.

00:20:39.840 --> 00:20:41.260
But it kills your state.

00:20:41.260 --> 00:20:42.180
BRETT MORGAN: Cool.

00:20:42.180 --> 00:20:44.670
So the reason I did this
is that I'm actually

00:20:44.670 --> 00:20:47.760
doing the idiomatic thing
when creating state.

00:20:47.760 --> 00:20:50.040
I'm creating a stateful object.

00:20:50.040 --> 00:20:54.490
And so HomePage is
a StatefulWidget.

00:20:54.490 --> 00:20:58.940
And it has a partner object
called HomePageState.

00:20:58.940 --> 00:21:01.370
And when this object
is created, it's

00:21:01.370 --> 00:21:04.640
turning around and making
a connection to Firestore.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:06.815
And we're doing a
query against Firestore

00:21:06.815 --> 00:21:09.500
where we're looking at the
collection of ice cream stores

00:21:09.500 --> 00:21:12.140
and ordering them by their name.

00:21:12.140 --> 00:21:15.140
Now that I've got this
query, I can actually

00:21:15.140 --> 00:21:18.830
turn around and start using it.

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:21.790
So what I'm doing is
I've got a StreamBuilder.

00:21:21.790 --> 00:21:25.290
I pass that StreamBuilder
to my stream.

00:21:25.290 --> 00:21:27.990
And a stream, for those
of you who aren't aware,

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:31.590
it's kind of the lovechild
of futures and lists

00:21:31.590 --> 00:21:34.980
where it's this list that
goes off into the future.

00:21:34.980 --> 00:21:38.500
And each future
state is actually

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:39.940
a state of the database.

00:21:39.940 --> 00:21:43.560
So someone makes a change
in the Cloud Firestore,

00:21:43.560 --> 00:21:45.480
adds another ice
cream store, it will

00:21:45.480 --> 00:21:47.010
turn around and update the app.

00:21:47.010 --> 00:21:50.347
And we'll go from 14
ice cream stores to 15.

00:21:50.347 --> 00:21:51.930
MATTHEW SULLIVAN:
So one of the things

00:21:51.930 --> 00:21:54.660
that people might see here is
this seems a little complicated

00:21:54.660 --> 00:21:55.953
with streams and asynchronous.

00:21:55.953 --> 00:21:57.120
What are they talking about?

00:21:57.120 --> 00:22:00.060
One of the strengths of Dart and
one of the strengths of Flutter

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:02.690
is it's asynchronous
in its very nature.

00:22:02.690 --> 00:22:04.500
It means you don't
have to spin up threads

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:07.290
to handle things like
network calls or whatnot

00:22:07.290 --> 00:22:11.020
or to handle like I/O basically.

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:13.440
And what you can do with it
is it's very easy for Brett

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:15.480
in a few lines of
code here to say,

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:17.940
here is the stream of
data from Firestore.

00:22:17.940 --> 00:22:19.440
Here is the StreamBuilder.

00:22:19.440 --> 00:22:21.750
And this app, with
a few lines of code,

00:22:21.750 --> 00:22:26.980
will update itself every
time that data store changes.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:29.970
So if we go in and we add
new things to the database,

00:22:29.970 --> 00:22:32.580
that's going to be reflected
in the app automatically.

00:22:32.580 --> 00:22:36.030
And it will rebuild itself with
no additional work from you.

00:22:36.030 --> 00:22:37.310
BRETT MORGAN: Yes.

00:22:37.310 --> 00:22:39.290
And of course, knowing
you, that you're all

00:22:39.290 --> 00:22:40.972
quite interested in
ice cream stores,

00:22:40.972 --> 00:22:42.680
I went ahead and did
the next step, which

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.920
is instead of just saying we've
got 14 stores, I turn around,

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:47.525
and I've created a ListView.

00:22:47.525 --> 00:22:49.460
And so we've got
this ListView here.

00:22:49.460 --> 00:22:51.740
We've got a bunch of stores.

00:22:51.740 --> 00:22:55.400
Here's a part of Dart
syntax that I quite like.

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:58.400
We have a thing called
named constructors.

00:22:58.400 --> 00:23:00.740
So I'm constructing an
object called ListView.

00:23:00.740 --> 00:23:02.930
But I'm constructing
it in a specific way

00:23:02.930 --> 00:23:04.580
where I give it a builder.

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:07.130
So I'm effectively
creating the ListTiles.

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:09.860
But I'm only creating them
on an as needs basis, which

00:23:09.860 --> 00:23:13.340
means that if I have, say,
a really long list of data,

00:23:13.340 --> 00:23:15.920
I only create the
appropriate parts that

00:23:15.920 --> 00:23:18.630
are being shown on the screen,
saving us CPU, saving us

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:19.130
memory.

00:23:24.570 --> 00:23:27.110
One of the great things
that Matt mentioned earlier

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:30.620
is that when you have all of
your Flutter code as code,

00:23:30.620 --> 00:23:32.810
you get the ability to
turn around and pull out

00:23:32.810 --> 00:23:35.000
abstractions and name things.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.160
So I've got this
ListView.builder,

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:39.410
but to me, I really
wanted to call it

00:23:39.410 --> 00:23:46.270
a StoreList, which means that
my build method for my state

00:23:46.270 --> 00:23:50.080
object now just goes,
OK, I've got a StoreList.

00:23:50.080 --> 00:23:51.850
And I've simplified
that complexity

00:23:51.850 --> 00:23:53.830
so that when I'm
reading this code later,

00:23:53.830 --> 00:23:55.330
I'm not turned
around and overloaded

00:23:55.330 --> 00:23:56.705
with a whole bunch
of stuff and I

00:23:56.705 --> 00:24:00.126
